    "https://centrecollege.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/Centre/job/Danville-Kentucky/Public-Safety-Officer_JR100238": {
      "title": "Public Safety Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin Centre College as we recruit exceptional faculty and staff! At Centre, we seek passionate and qualified individuals committed to fostering academic excellence, vibrant community, and inclusive growth. We offer competitive compensation, comprehensive benefits, and ongoing professional development. Our collegial environment values diversity of perspectives, encourages meaningful discourse and celebrates the unique contributions of each community member. Reports directly to the Director of Public Safety and is responsible for providing basic security functions, enforcing campus rules and regulations, conducting fire and safety inspections, enforcing parking and traffic regulations and responding to calls for assistance.  This person is a member of the Student Life staff reporting to the Vice President for Student Life through the Director of Public Safety. The position will be a hybrid of evening shift two days a week and night shift two days a week. 4p-2a two days 9p-7a two days Essential Job Functions: Enforce all College rules and regulations, as well as, local, state and Federal laws; Responsible for building security and periodic building checks; Conduct routine fire and safety inspections on campus; Investigate and report on incidents, offenses, injury/illnesses and non-injury motor vehicle accidents; Patrol the campus on foot and by using various vehicles including bicycles, automobiles and golf carts; Conduct fire drills in all residence halls each semester; Respond to calls for assistance including unlocks, motorist assists, suspicious and criminal activity and after-hours maintenance requests; Maintain the campus fire extinguisher program; Communicate with the Danville Police Department regarding crimes occurring on or near campus; Enforce parking and traffic regulations; Proper maintenance of all department equipment; Working special events; Demonstrate an appreciation for and sensitivity to a diverse academic community, fostering an inclusive environment for students, faculty, and staff from a variety of social, economic, cultural, ideological, racial, and ethnic backgrounds. Marginal Job Functions: Contribute to the overall success of the department by performing all other essential duties and responsibilities as assigned. Education and Experience High school diploma or equivalent required.Some college courses preferred. Training in inter-cultural competency preferred. Related work experience, in the areas of safety and security. Valid driver’s license. Knowledge and Skills Good oral, written and interpersonal communications skills. Ability to function collaboratively within a diverse and dynamic setting. Ability to work with little supervision. Willingness to work a flexible schedule, which will include evening, weekend and holiday hours. Computer skills necessary. Physical Requirements: Limited pushing, pulling, lifting.Lifting would not exceed 20 lbs. Mobility on campus necessary. Centre provides a supportive, engaging setting where faculty and staff thrive as we advance our shared mission: preparing students for lives of learning, leadership, and service. Join our community and leave a lasting impact on generations to come. As a fundamental policy and in compliance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and all other applicable non-discrimination laws, Centre College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or gender expression, age, disability, veteran’s status, or genetic information in the administration of any of its education or employment policies. Centre College hires and promotes its employees and admits its students on the basis of merit, qualification, and character."
    },
    "https://centrecollege.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/Centre/job/Facilities-Management-Bldg/General-Services_JR100087": {
      "title": "General Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin Centre College as we recruit exceptional faculty and staff! At Centre, we seek passionate and qualified individuals committed to fostering academic excellence, vibrant community, and inclusive growth. We offer competitive compensation, comprehensive benefits, and ongoing professional development. Our collegial environment values diversity of perspectives, encourages meaningful discourse and celebrates the unique contributions of each community member. Under the general supervision of the General Services Leader, the General Services Technician performs a variety of manual, operational, and support duties for Facilities Management. This position plays a critical role in supporting campus operations through event setup and breakdown, furniture moving and storage, building maintenance assistance, material deliveries, gutter cleaning, and other general services functions that contribute to a safe, efficient, and welcoming campus environment. Duties are performed independently and as part of a team. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Event and Campus Support Set up, arrange, and remove tables, chairs, staging, and other equipment for campus events, meetings, and special functions. Assist the Special Events Facilitator with the setup and breakdown of large campus events. Transport, load, unload, and assemble furniture and equipment as needed. Provide support for faculty, staff, student, and community events to ensure timely and successful event execution. Furniture, Storage, and Delivery Operations Receive, verify, store, inventory, and distribute furniture, equipment, and supplies. Maintain organized storage areas and inventory records for furniture and related materials. Install, relocate, and remove furniture in residence halls, offices, classrooms, and other campus facilities. Operate light and medium-duty vehicles to transport materials, furniture, equipment, and supplies throughout campus. Assist with shipping, receiving, and warehouse operations to support Facilities Management activities. Deliver materials and supplies to campus departments in response to work order requests. Building and Grounds Maintenance Clean and maintain gutters, downspouts, rooftops, and other building areas by removing leaves, debris, and obstructions. Perform seasonal maintenance activities to help protect campus buildings and infrastructure. Support pest control activities and coordinate access for outside contractors and service providers as required. Provide assistance to the grounds crew with emergency response activities during inclement weather and other campus emergencies. Fleet and Equipment Support Maintain and distribute annual vehicle registration and insurance documentation for fleet vehicles. Assist with minor vehicle service needs, including jump-starting vehicles when necessary. Operate forklifts, man lifts, and other equipment safely and efficiently after receiving appropriate training. Pick up parts, supplies, and materials needed by Facilities Management staff and trades personnel. Vendor and Operational Support Coordinate with contracted vendors and service providers, including pest control, elevator maintenance, fire alarm service, and other contractors requiring campus access. Provide timely access to restricted areas when a college representative is required. Assist with mail, package, and material deliveries between campus locations and external facilities. Safety and Compliance Follow all College safety policies, procedures, and training requirements. Utilize proper personal protective equipment (PPE) and safe work practices at all times. Maintain a safe and professional work environment. Perform duties in a manner that supports the College's commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. Marginal Duties Perform trash and recycling support duties for Facilities Management shop areas. Assist other Facilities Management departments as workload and operational needs require. Perform additional duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Education and Experience High school diploma or GED required. Valid driver's license with an acceptable driving record required. Experience in event setup, material handling, moving services, warehouse operations, facilities maintenance, or related work preferred. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to work independently and collaboratively with a team. Ability to follow written and verbal instructions. Strong customer service and communication skills. Ability to organize work, prioritize tasks, and meet deadlines. Ability to operate or learn to operate forklifts, man lifts, and other equipment safely. Ability to interact professionally with students, faculty, staff, vendors, and visitors. Physical Requirements Ability to perform strenuous physical labor for extended periods. Frequent standing, walking, bending, stooping, kneeling, climbing stairs and ladders, and working at heights. Ability to lift, carry, push, and pull materials weighing 50 pounds or more. Ability to work indoors and outdoors in various weather conditions. Mobility throughout campus is required. Essential Employee Requirement Facilities Management provides critical services necessary for the safety and operation of the College. Employees in this position are considered essential personnel and may be required to report to work during inclement weather, emergencies, and other situations that impact campus operations. Centre provides a supportive, engaging setting where faculty and staff thrive as we advance our shared mission: preparing students for lives of learning, leadership, and service. Join our community and leave a lasting impact on generations to come. As a fundamental policy and in compliance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and all other applicable non-discrimination laws, Centre College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or gender expression, age, disability, veteran’s status, or genetic information in the administration of any of its education or employment policies. Centre College hires and promotes its employees and admits its students on the basis of merit, qualification, and character."
    },
    "https://centrecollege.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/Centre/job/Young-Hall/Life-Sciences-Technician_JR100244": {
      "title": "Life Sciences Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin Centre College as we recruit exceptional faculty and staff! At Centre, we seek passionate and qualified individuals committed to fostering academic excellence, vibrant community, and inclusive growth. We offer competitive compensation, comprehensive benefits, and ongoing professional development. Our collegial environment values diversity of perspectives, encourages meaningful discourse and celebrates the unique contributions of each community member. Centre College invites applications for the position of Life Sciences Technician in the Division of Science and Mathematics. This position plays a vital role in supporting undergraduate instruction, faculty research, animal welfare, and greenhouse operations. The successful candidate will provide daily care and husbandry for a diverse collection of teaching and research animals, prepare laboratories for Life Sciences courses, support faculty and student research, supervise student workers, and manage greenhouse operations during the summer months . Please submit resume/CV and cover letter with application. We will begin reviewing applications in October 2026, with the goal of filling the position by November 30, 2026 . Responsible for animal care and husbandry for the Life Sciences Academic Programs,  Life Sciences laboratory preparation, and Greenhouse Management during the summer.  Individual is responsible for supervising undergraduate assistants in these three areas.  Reports to the Chair of the Division of Science and Mathematics.     Primary responsibilities will be in the following three areas:   1. Animal Care and Husbandry Provide daily care and follow all daily standard operating procedures associated with laboratory animals in the Biology, and Biochemistry/Molecular Biology, Neuroscience, and Psychology programs following OLAW regulations; Inspect animal housing and testing rooms and maintain close contact with IACUC Chair to ensure compliance with federal standards and regulations; Maintain animal census and animal care and housekeeping records; Maintain breeding stock and breed as needed for classes or research needs Maintain and perform minor repair of laboratory equipment and animal enclosures; Order, stock, distribute, and maintain inventory of animal laboratory supplies; pick up supplies from local businesses as needed; Work with faculty to ensure animal species needed for teaching are available; Daily inspections to maintain general health of all animals; Notify Instrument & Laboratory Technician or Facilities Management about matters concerning maintenance such as water leaks, plugged drains, power outages, etc. in the animal care areas; Work with faculty regarding research needs, help design equipment needed to implement research needs while accommodating the health and care of the animals being used during the experiments; Perform animal care and husbandry functions necessary to maintain animal welfare during periods when student workers are unavailable, including weekends, holidays, and academic breaks.Train student workers in the proper care and handling of  animals with which they will be working; Supervise student workers and student research assistants; Supervise students in feeding, watering and routine husbandry of avian, rodent, reptile and fish species used in both research and teaching; Supervise students in room sanitation and cage, tank, and equipment cleaning; Supervise student workers in performing tasks associated with maintaining avian and rodent breeding colonies; Supervise students during research to insure that animals are maintained properly. Notify researchers if there is a health concern posed during an experiment; Remove all animal waste. Serve on the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee: participate in semi-annual meetings, review research protocol applications, inspect facilitites, review animal use programs.   2. Life Science Laboratory Preparation   Prepare materials for weekly introductory level Biochemistry/Molecular Biology lab (BMB 210L) (2-3 lab sections per long term); Set up and take down weekly BMB 210L labs (materials and equipment); Order and maintain equipment and supplies for BMB 210L; Order and maintain the chemicals and glassware in the BMB prep room Help the BIO 110 Lab Coordinator with the ordering and stocking of the general biology stock room; Order and maintain equipment and supplies for the Environmental Studies, Neuroscience, and Psychology Programs; Order fish and maintain tanks for PSY 110 during the time frame of the experiment. Co-ordinate with the instructors; Provide rats for the PSY 305 lab. Inspect and keep records on the health and notify instructor with issues pertaining to the animals health, intervene if necessary to insure the well being of the animals being used in the lab; Supervise undergraduate laboratory assistants.     3. Seasonal Greenhouse Management ( Summer- two months)   Co-ordinate with BIO 110 Lab Instructor & Lab Coordinator as to the start and stop date of summer greenhouse management; Ensure proper growth conditions for greenhouse; Maintain supplies and basic equipment; Notify Instrument & Laboratory Technician or Facilities Management about matters concerning maintenance; Hire, train, and supervise greenhouse assistants and work study students, and take care of greenhouse tasks when necessary to maintain proper growth conditions.      Marginal Job Functions:   Contribute to the overall success of the department by performing all other essential duties and responsibilities as assigned.   Requirements:   Education and Experience   o   Bachelors degree in Life Sciences required; o   Laboratory Animal Technician (LAT) or Laboratory Animal Technologist (LATG) certification through the American Association for Laboratory Animal Science (AALAS) is preferred. o   Expected to maintain knowledge of current guidelines for the care and use of animals in research and education             Knowledge and Skills                   o   Chemical inventory management o   Laboratory safety procedures o   Biohazard handling o   OSHA compliance o   Equipment troubleshooting o   Knowledge of solutions preparations o   Valid driver’s license necessary as travel by car or truck to transport live animals and maintain food and bedding supplies. o   Ability to work well and problem solve independently while also working closely with faculty in the life sciences o  Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications and ability to learn institutional software systems             Physical Requirements: o   Ability to safely lift and move materials weighing up to 50 pounds with or without reasonable accommodation.Working with, taking specific precautions against, and being immunized against potentially hazardous agents. Centre provides a supportive, engaging setting where faculty and staff thrive as we advance our shared mission: preparing students for lives of learning, leadership, and service. Join our community and leave a lasting impact on generations to come. As a fundamental policy and in compliance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and all other applicable non-discrimination laws, Centre College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or gender expression, age, disability, veteran’s status, or genetic information in the administration of any of its education or employment policies. Centre College hires and promotes its employees and admits its students on the basis of merit, qualification, and character."
    }
